# Wow, Rusty, what a terrible templating system!

INTRO_PARA1="$(bold Century Metadata) is a project to provide storage for small amounts
of auxiliary data.  As an example, this is useful for Bitcoin wallets,
which can be restored from 12 seed words, but cannot know about more
complex funds without additional data.  On restore, your wallet would attempt to
fetch this data from $(url https://centurymetadata.org) or a mirror."

INTRO_PARA2="We are currently in alpha, seeking feedback."

FILE_FORMAT_PARA1="The $(url "file format" SPECIFICATION.md#file-format) is designed to be
self-explanatory and use standard, long-lived primitives as much as
possible.  Every file contains a text preamble which describes the
format exactly, followed by the encrypted data."

FILE_FORMAT_PARA2="The data itself is a series of NUL-separated type, name, contents
triples.  Obviously this cannot be validated on the production server,
but the test server (which only allows known keys) will check the file
is compliant."

BITCOIN_PARA1="The BIP 32 path recommended for centurymetadata is $(literal 0x44315441\'/N\'/0\')
through $(literal /3\') ($(literal DATA)), where $(literal N) starts at 0 and increments for each
additional key set.  $(literal /0\') is the writer secp256k1 key, $(literal /1\') is the
reader secp256k1 key, $(literal /2\') seeds the writer ML-KEM-1024 key
(currently unused), and $(literal /3\') seeds the reader ML-KEM-1024 key.  The
READER_ID field is hashed, but if you share your reader keys others
could also send encrypted data to you: files created with your own
writer keys can be trusted."

BITCOIN_PARA2="The types of records accepted are:"

BITCOIN_TYPE1="Type: $(literal bitcoin psbt), a base64-encoded PSBT for wallet to sign"

BITCOIN_TYPE2="Type: $(literal bitcoin transaction), a hex-encoded transaction for wallet to broadcast"

BITCOIN_TYPE3="Type: $(literal bitcoin output script descriptor), a wallet descriptor string to wallet to find funds"

BITCOIN_TYPE4="Type: $(literal bitcoin wallet labels), a BIP-329 JSONL to annotate wallet"

BITCOIN_TYPE5="Type: $(literal next cmdata derivation path), another $(literal N) value for chaining more than one file."

BITCOIN_PARA3="The detailed requirements are $(url "specified here" SPECIFICATION.md#individual-record-formats)."

API_PARA1="The test API endpoint can be found at $(url testapi.centurymetadata.org https://testapi.centurymetadata.org/api/v1)."

API_AUTHORIZE="Entry Creation: POST /api/v1/authorize/{READER_ID}/{WRITER}/{AUTHTOKEN}"

API_AUTHORIZE_DESC="You need to get an $(bold AUTHTOKEN) for each new entry.  There can only be
one entry for any $(bold READER_ID)/$(bold WRITER) pair, but once the entry is
authorized it can be updated by the writer at any time.  $(bold READER_ID) is
the 64-character hex encoding of SHA256(secp_pubkey|mlkem_pubkey)."

API_UPDATE="Entry Update: POST /api/v1/update"

API_UPDATE_DESC="Updates a previously authorized writer/reader entry.  The
$(literal Content-Type: application/x-centurymetadata) should contain a valid
centurymetadata file."

API_LISTBUNDLES="Listing Bundles: GET /api/v1/listbundles"

API_LISTBUNDLES_DESC_PARA1="Records are stored in a two-level hierarchy of directories and bundles, each bundle holding up to 1024 records.  To find your bundle, fetch the bundle listing."

API_LISTBUNDLES_DESC_PARA2="Returns a JSON array of objects, each giving the $(literal directory) and $(literal bundle) names (hex-range identifiers) and the bundle's 0-based $(literal index) within its directory: the bit position to set for $(literal fetchxor)."

API_FETCHXOR="Retrieving Entries: POST /api/v1/fetchxor/{DIRECTORY}"

API_FETCHXOR_DESC_PARA1="POST a 128-byte bitmask (one bit per bundle in the directory) as $(literal Content-Type: application/octet-stream). The server XORs together every bundle whose bit is set and returns the result as $(literal Content-Type: application/x-centurymetadata): always 1024 x 8192 = 8,388,608 bytes.  Setting a single bit simply returns that bundle."

API_FETCHXOR_DESC_PARA2="For $(bold private) retrieval, query two servers with complementary bitmasks (one with your bundle's bit set, one with it cleared, all other bits matching): XOR the two responses together to recover your bundle without either server learning which one you wanted."

KNOWNKEYS_PARA1="Because it holds the corresponding private keys, the test server at
$(url testapi.centurymetadata.org https://testapi.centurymetadata.org/api/v1) can decrypt
and check compliance for a small, fixed set of $(bold known) reader identities, rejecting
$(literal authorize)/$(literal update) requests for any other $(literal READER_ID). A reader
identity is $(bold known) if it is derived (as above) from a BIP-39 mnemonic consisting of
the same word repeated 12 times whose checksum happens to be valid: roughly 1 in 16 of the
2048-word English wordlist qualifies (130 words). The test server computes this set
itself from the public wordlist; there is no secret list to request access to."

KNOWNKEYS_PARA2="Ordering these known words by their position in the wordlist, the first
half are the $(bold normal) case: the record's $(literal WRITER_PUBKEY) must match that same
identity's own derived writer key, i.e. the data is self-authored. The second half are
reserved for the test server's own pre-populated example data, and may use other writers."

KNOWNKEYS_PARA3="For convenience when testing your own implementation, the first few known
words (ordered by wordlist position) are $(literal action), $(literal agent), $(literal aim);
the last few are $(literal word), $(literal world), $(literal yellow). The full,
authoritative list is the checked-in
$(url known_words.txt https://raw.githubusercontent.com/rustyrussell/centurymetadata/master/python/centurymetadata/server/known_words.txt)."

TOOLS_PARA1="There is an experimental Python package to encode and decode
centurymetadata files in the $(url "GitHub repository" https://github.com/rustyrussell/centurymetadata)"

ROADMAP_PARA1="I'm currently raising startup funding.  If successful, I will use that to start a non-profit foundation to raise a long-term endowment to ensure the longevity of the data."

HOW_MUCH_PARA1="There will never be a charge for ratelimited updates or retrievals;
the idea is to charge a small cost for the creation of new entries to
cover ongoing running costs.  We may also accept donations."

WHO_PARA1="Rusty Russell started this as a side project; my original problem was
how to give someone timelocked bitcoin, but realized there was a large
related class of problems for someone to solve."

FEEDBACK_PARA1="Advice, suggestions, kudos, blame: hosting is on $(url GitHub https://github.com/rustyrussell/centurymetadata), and you can reach us on $(url Twitter https://twitter.com/centurymetadata), or send
$(url "me email" mailto:rusty@centurymetadata.org) or other contact as listed on 
